- 2026-09-07 [**PARTLY SUPERSEDED BY THE NEXT ENTRY** ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ read both. Wrong in this line: `codex doctor` DOES report a malformed role (as a `startup warning` at line 121; the "printed nothing" claim came from reading only doctor's first 30 lines); it is SIX SQLite DBs under a redirected CODEX_HOME, not five; "beside the built-in default/worker" is retracted, those names are partly confabulated; and the plugin cache is ELEVEN releases behind, not seven. The central finding ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ auto-discovery ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ stands.] Codex agent-role discovery VERIFIED (branch worktree-codex-agent-role-discovery, stacked on the unmerged PR #18; docs + evidence only, NO code change and none needed). `.codex/agents/*.toml` is a DISCOVERED DIRECTORY, not a manifest: codex-setup emits seven role files and no `[agents]` block, and a real `codex exec` on 0.153.4 listed all seven ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ doc-gardener, evaluator, explorer, generator, planner, reviewer, risk-classifier ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ beside the built-in default/worker. The oracle had to be a DELIBERATELY MALFORMED role file, because a well-formed one that is silently ignored is indistinguishable from one that loaded: a file with `name`+`description` and no `developer_instructions`, referenced by nothing, produced "Ignoring malformed agent role definition: agent role file at .../probeB.toml must define `developer_instructions`" ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ the loader reading a file it was never pointed at. Arms A and C are the controls that prove the oracle can speak. THREE keepers: `codex doctor` printed nothing at all for that same broken role while `codex exec` warned (second time doctor cannot answer "is my .codex/ doing anything"); a malformed role is a WARNING, not a fatal, so unlike the V3 config.toml defects it degrades silently and takes nothing down with it; and `[agents.<name>]` config declarations are a separate, WEAKER path ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a missing `config_file` warns, a malformed DECLARED file does not, so content is validated on discovery and not on declaration. The item is SPLIT: the agent half closes, the `--user` hooks half stays open but is no longer blocked ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ `CODEX_HOME` redirects the whole user layer (measured: doctor put config, log dir and all five SQLite DBs under an empty throwaway home and failed only on the missing-auth check), so it needs a scratch home plus auth rather than a machine-wide write; copying a live token is the operator's call, and the home must not sit under the system temp dir. Two self-inflicted failures, both now in AGENT_NOTES: run 1 was piped through `head` and died to SIGPIPE two arms early with a results file that merely looked short (the same signal family this repo has ratcheted twice, self-inflicted and paid for in model calls), and arm F's first attempt never reached Codex because a bare `harness/codex-setup.sh` falls through to the ~/.claude/plugins cache ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ 0.2.9 from 2026-08-12, predating codex-setup ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ unless HARNESS_ENGINE is exported. Evidence: state/evidence/2026-09-07-codex-agent-role-discovery/. Same day, a /harness-doctor run found nothing red and filed two new fix_plan items: the repo's own gate is all-null in harness.config.json while its 300+/290+ suite sits unwired, and the installed plugin cache is seven versions stale.
- 2026-09-07 (same task, after the fresh-context review) Codex agent-role finding CORRECTED and completed. The review returned FIX-THEN-SHIP with two blockers and five should-fixes, and taking them overturned one of the round-1 claims. WHAT WAS WRONG: round 1 asserted in six surfaces that `codex doctor` is blind to a malformed agent role. The reviewer refused it as uncited (no committed doctor output existed anywhere, and the script comment attributed it to a different arm than the prose did). Writing the missing arm showed the claim is FALSE ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ doctor carries the identical text as a `startup warning` field at line 121 of its output; the original had piped doctor through `sed -n '1,30p'` and read only the first 30 lines. That is the 2026-09-06 ratchet ("a filtered grep cannot prove a claim of silence") recurring one day later, in the session that had just read it. Now re-ratcheted in AGENTS.md as a CITATION requirement rather than an exhortation: a foreign-tool fact in prose names the results file and line, and the re-runnable probe contains the arm that produced it. TWO MORE ROUND-1 OVERREACHES, both fixed by measuring: "spawnable" and "a generated agent role in use" were inferences from an enumeration of names, so round 3 arm H1 spawned a discovered, undeclared role whose developer_instructions carry XYZZY-ROLE-TOKEN-4417 ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a string in exactly one file on disk ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ and got it back verbatim, which is what actually closes the fix_plan clause; and "beside the built-in default and worker" was wrong, because arm G3 asked the same question twice against an empty project layer and got `general-purpose` both times, while other arms volunteered `default`/`explorer`/`worker` with no such files present, so the enumeration is partly confabulated and only the loader warning plus the spawn token are load-bearing. Also corrected: six SQLite DBs under a redirected CODEX_HOME, not five; the CODEX_HOME and temp-dir-refusal quotes now have committed artifacts (arms G2 and H2); the stale banner at the top of codex-setup.md that still listed agent-TOML discovery as not re-run; two guard-probe controls that printed a reassuring line when the file was MISSING (grep's "No such file" also exits non-zero) ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ and the same defect reproduced by hand in a new script an hour later, because `codex doctor` exits non-zero on its auth check and under pipefail that status beats a MATCHING grep, so the `||` fallback fires on success. Capture into a variable, then assert. Probe arms that cost model calls are now behind PROBE_SKIP_MODEL=1 so the pass/fail logic can be re-verified for free. Forward pointers added to the two older evidence READMEs that still called the question open. Gate after the fixes: bash 325/0, PS 5.1 333/0.
- 2026-09-07 (same task, second fresh-context review) FIX-THEN-SHIP with four blockers; all taken, and one was a PRIVACY LEAK the repo's own guard could not see. B1: two committed transcripts carried the real OS username in Codex's JSON-ESCAPED form, `C://Users//<name>//AppData//...`. That matches neither the scrubber's single-separator rules nor the pre-commit denylist `[Uu]sers[///]<name>`, whose character class consumes exactly one separator and then expects the name ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ so the guard passed the commit. Fixed three ways: the scrubber gained a rule matching ANY run of separators; scrub-proof.sh's fixture now plants single, double and triple escapings; and the proof asserts on the USERNAME rather than on the guard's own pattern, since an assertion borrowing the guard's blind spot inherits it. The file list was also replaced by a `results-*.txt` glob after it silently skipped the two newest transcripts. Ratcheted in AGENTS.md. B3 was the same family in a different dress: the README told readers to set PROBE_SKIP_MODEL=1 to re-run "only the free parts", but only 2 of 5 model-calling scripts read the variable ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ 7 of 9 paid arms fired anyway, so following the documentation cost real money. All scripts now honour it, proved by skip-proof.sh, which shadows `codex` on PATH with a stub that fails loudly on `exec` AND carries a negative control that fires when the switch is off (without it the proof would pass on a script that never calls codex at all). Also ratcheted. B2: the previous PROGRESS line still asserted the overturned doctor claim plus three other stale numbers; it now carries an inline SUPERSEDED marker rather than relying on a later entry, because these lines are read individually by grep. B4: the correction commit had itself introduced an uncited foreign-tool claim ("`codex agents` is a session browser") in the same change that ratcheted against uncited claims ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ now backed by captured `codex agents --help` output, along with the `struct AgentRoleToml with 3 elements` field list read from the installed binary. THE SPAWN CLAIM ALSO GOT ITS MISSING CONTROL. Round 3 showed a discovered role returning a token unique to its own file, but its prompt did not forbid file reads and the file sat in the cwd, so a parent that simply read it gives an identical transcript. Round 4b: reads forbidden, one role file, two placements ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ in `.codex/agents/` the token comes back, and the SAME bytes in `.codex/agents-off/` return NO-SUCH-ROLE. The token is on disk in both arms and returns in only one, so discovery is the delivery mechanism, not a file read. Honest residue recorded rather than papered over: no single arm both spawns and uses a file the generator itself wrote (the spawn probes use a hand-written role so the token can be unique), so the "generated role in use" clause is closed by three arms in combination. Gate: bash 325/0, PS 5.1 333/0, parse-check 11/11, skip-proof and scrub-proof both PASS in both directions.
- 2026-09-08 Guard-hook twin gap CLOSED (branch worktree-guard-hook-twin-gap, plugin 0.3.9 -> 0.4.0). `block-destructive.sh` now carries the four PowerShell/cmd.exe destructive forms the `.ps1` twin has shipped with since the start: `Remove-Item` with `-Recurse`/`-Force`, `rd`/`rmdir` with the recursive switch, the cmd.exe recursive/quiet delete switches, and `Format-Volume`/`Clear-Disk`/`Clear-Content`. The gap was NOT Windows-only cosmetics: `run.mjs` dispatches `.ps1` on win32 and `.sh` everywhere else, the PowerShell tool runs on POSIX via pwsh (the `.sh` file's own spec-lock block already says so and already matched `Remove-Item` there), and the `.sh` hook is the one Codex loads under its shell-tool matcher ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ so on POSIX an agent driving pwsh could issue a recursive delete and match nothing in the always-on denylist. THE PROOF IS THE CONTRAST, and it is committed as one re-runnable three-arm script plus its unfiltered output rather than as prose: arm 1 runs every case against the hook at the pinned pre-fix SHA 1621ae7 and all seventeen return rc=0 ALLOWED ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a wrong VALUE, not an exception, which is the 2026-09-06 ratchet's test for whether a mutation check proves anything; arm 2 is the fixed hook, all seventeen DENIED; arm 3 is the `.ps1` twin agreeing case for case. Arm 2b exists because the same ratchet says a guardrail predicate is unverified until it has run over a real input at real size: a new pattern inside a 190,920-byte payload whose bulk follows a newline (grep cannot exit early until it has read a whole line, and only an early exit can kill the writer with SIGPIPE), run both plain and against a copy of the hook with `set -euo pipefail` injected, with a benign control at the same size that must still pass ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ otherwise DENIED would prove only that something in a 190 KB payload trips the guard. Assertions: 39 new on the bash side and 38 on the PowerShell side (thirty-one denials plus eight negative controls per twin), where only `Remove-Item` had been pinned ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ three of the four `.ps1` patterns had been shipping unasserted, so the twin that was CORRECT was the one with the weaker test coverage. ONE SHARED FALSE POSITIVE KEPT DELIBERATELY: prose quoting the cmd.exe quiet-delete literal is now denied on the bash side too. The `.ps1` twin already denied it, so this is the twins converging rather than a new defect, and narrowing it would mean diverging from shipped behaviour inside the most safety-critical file in the repo. It bit within the hour ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ the first attempt at this change's own commit message was blocked by the pattern the change adds, resolved the way AGENT_NOTES already prescribes (write to a file, `git commit -F`). Recorded in the evidence README and in AGENT_NOTES rather than papered over. Plugin version bumped per the 2026-08-12 ratchet: a shipped hook's BEHAVIOUR changed and consumers get that hook from the plugin cache. Cross-surface sweep found and fixed the 2026-09-06 evidence README that still called the gap open (forward pointer added). Gate: bash 364/0 (was 325), PS 5.1 371/0 (was 333). Evidence: state/evidence/2026-09-08-guard-hook-twin-gap/.
- 2026-09-08 (same task, after the fresh-context review) SHIP with five should-fixes, all taken, and one of them was a REAL OVER-BLOCK I had shipped and then documented as acceptable. The first version ported the two cmd.exe patterns exactly as the `.ps1` had always written them ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ switch matched ADJACENT to the command ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ and that shape is wrong in BOTH directions at once. Over-blocking: on POSIX it denies `rmdir /srv/cache`, `rmdir /sys/fs/cgroup/x`, `rd /storage/tmp`, because an absolute path whose first segment starts with the switch letter looks like the switch; `/srv /sys /sbin /snap /share /storage` are ordinary roots, `rmdir` there cannot even delete a non-empty directory, and the `.sh` hook is precisely the one that runs on POSIX ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ so it is a harmless command denied on the platform the file serves. Under-blocking: the same shape ALLOWED `rmdir /q /s build` and `del /f /s *.log` on BOTH twins, real recursive deletes that an adjacent-only match misses when the flags are ordered the other way. Fixed in both twins in one diff by requiring the switch to be a standalone token ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ `[^|]*` before it so flag order does not matter, a space-or-end boundary after it so a path cannot impersonate it ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ with the two flag-order denials and the three POSIX-path controls now pinned per twin. THE METHOD LESSON, now ratcheted: I wrote "one shared false positive, kept deliberately" from PROSE EXAMPLES and cited it in four surfaces; a differential probe over real command vocabulary found three more, all of which mattered more than the one I had named. An FP list assembled from examples always undercounts. Second lesson in the same finding: the `.ps1` twin was the one with CORRECT behaviour on the four patterns and the WEAKER tests ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ only `Remove-Item` was ever asserted there, which is how its own over-block survived unnoticed for months. When closing a parity gap, count assertions on both sides, not just patterns. Three further fixes: the probe defaulted its pre-fix ref to `origin/main`, which would have made arm 1 print eleven DENIED lines the moment this branch merged and turned the committed results file into a self-contradiction (now pinned to the SHA 1621ae7); the arm-2b benign case was labelled a "positive control" when it is a negative one (this repo reserves "positive control" for the arm that proves the oracle can speak); and a test comment claimed all fixtures were fragment-built to avoid tripping the guard while four cmd.exe literals sat verbatim ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ the reviewer hit that guard three times writing the review. Also corrected: the arm-2b payload is 190,920 bytes, not 190,920 (my figure counted the filler and dropped the destructive first line), and the same artifact was called "150 KB" in a script comment. Two twin divergences were measured and recorded rather than fixed, both in the safe direction: exotic Unicode separators (denied by sh, allowed by ps1, and not real cmd.exe separators) and newlines inside a command (allowed by sh, denied by ps1, inherited by five pre-existing patterns, and not valid PowerShell on the permissive side). Final gate: bash 364/0, PS 5.1 371/0.
- 2026-09-08 (same task, SECOND fresh-context review) FIX-THEN-SHIP with one BLOCKER, and the blocker was that MY FIX FOR THE PREVIOUS REVIEW'S FINDING HAD OPENED A REAL BYPASS. Round 1 found the ported cmd.exe pattern over-blocked POSIX paths; the obvious repair ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ require a space-or-end boundary after the switch so a path cannot impersonate it ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ is wrong, because cmd.exe accepts CONCATENATED switches and the character after the switch is then `/`, not whitespace. Six forms that the crude original DENIED were ALLOWED by the careful replacement: `rd /s/q <dir>`, `rmdir /s/q <dir>`, `rd /q/s <dir>`, `del /s/q <dir>`, `cmd /c rd /s/q <dir>`, and a trailing switch followed straight by `&&`. The reviewer LIVE-FIRED them in real cmd.exe on Windows 11: `rd /s/q <dir>` deleted a populated three-level tree and `del /s/q` deleted files at two depths, both exit 0 ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ and `cmd /c rd /s/q` is the exact phrasing an agent uses from the Bash tool on Windows. A branch whose entire purpose was to CLOSE a guard gap was one review away from shipping a NET LOSS of coverage on the most safety-critical file in the repo. Fixed by making the boundary a character CLASS, `([[:space:];&"/]|$)`, plus `[qs/]*` on the rd arm so a leading quiet switch is absorbed; del's `[a-z]*` deliberately NOT widened, since the separator in the class already catches both concatenation orders and widening it would deny ordinary POSIX paths. Seventeen destructive forms now deny on both twins, six controls still pass, and all six bypass forms are pinned per twin.
  THE RATCHET, and it is the expensive one: A FIX FOR AN OVER-BLOCK IS ITSELF A LOOSENING, SO IT GETS DIFFERENTIALLY DIFFED AGAINST ITS PREDECESSOR OVER EVERY FORM THE OLD PATTERN CAUGHT. A pattern that is merely better REASONED than the one it replaces is not therefore safer, and I had reasoned carefully and shipped a hole. Corollary already in the rule: probe a foreign shell's pattern against that shell's own PARSER, not against how the flag is usually written. The AGENTS.md ratchet written in round 2 had to be rewritten too, because it PRESCRIBED the defective boundary ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a ratchet that teaches the next reader the bypass is worse than none.
  Six should-fixes also taken, all in the doc layer and all the same class: round-2 edits had updated SOME facts in a line and left others, so surfaces asserted a mix of true and false. Fixed across fix_plan, PROGRESS, AGENT_NOTES, both probe scripts and the 2026-09-06 evidence README: three citation ranges that no longer resolved after the results file was regenerated, four counts left at their round-1 values (nine/ten/eleven where it is now seventeen/twenty-one), an arm-1 description still saying `origin/main` three lines above the text recording that it had been pinned to a SHA, and a probe comment claiming its fixtures were fragment-built when the cmd.exe literals are verbatim. Final gate: bash 364/0 (was 325), PS 5.1 371/0 (was 333) ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ 39 new assertions on the bash side, 38 on the PowerShell side.
- 2026-09-08 (same task, THIRD fresh-context review) SHIP, and the differential it ran is the reassuring part: over a generated 12,960-form corpus the set "denied by the previous pattern, allowed by this one" is EMPTY, so round 3 was a strict superset and the two-rounds-running regression did not happen a third time. But it still found two REAL bypasses by live-fire, both pre-existing and missed by every generation including the long-shipped .ps1: a switch run LED BY ANOTHER FLAG (`del /f/s/q <dir>`, the canonical Windows build-script idiom ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ deleted files at two depths) and the NO-SPACE form (`rd/s/q <dir>` ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ removed a populated tree). It also found that the boundary CLASS had created a fresh false-positive family: any text carrying the word del beside a path segment ending in s or q, so `node del.js --out /logs/` was denied. So the FP accounting that had just been corrected was wrong again, for the third time, and for the same reason each time ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ it was written from examples rather than measured.
  THE FIX IS A CHANGE OF METHOD, not another boundary tweak: write the rule against cmd.exe's switch GRAMMAR. A switch is a run of one-letter `/x` segments, it may sit anywhere, and the separating space is optional ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ `/b(rd|rmdir)/b([^|]*[[:space:]])?(/[a-z])*/s(/[a-z])*([[:space:];&/"]|$)` and the del twin. The NEGATIVES were live-fired too, which is what licenses the narrowness: `rd /sq`, `rd /qs` and `rd /s/build` are all REFUSED by cmd.exe itself ("Parameter format not correct", "Invalid switch", target survived every time), so matching them would buy no coverage and cost over-blocking. Thirty-one destructive forms now deny on both twins; eight controls pass, including the two-letter tokens and the del-beside-a-path family, pinned so the accounting cannot drift again.
  FOUR ATTEMPTS, THREE WRONG, and the shape of the error was identical each time: reasoning about where the switch USUALLY sits instead of enumerating what the parser ACCEPTS. Adjacent-only missed flag order; space-or-end let concatenation through; a boundary class let a leading flag through and over-blocked prose. Ratcheted in AGENTS.md as clause (d), and the ratchet had to be rewritten for the SECOND time because its previous wording prescribed the boundary character that clause (d) disproves ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a ratchet that teaches the next reader the bypass is worse than none. Also fixed: the probe's control header covered a case that prints DENIED by design (an operator reads stdout, not the design doc). Final gate: bash 364/0 (was 325), PS 5.1 371/0 (was 333).
- 2026-09-08 (same task, FOURTH fresh-context review) FIX-THEN-SHIP, one BLOCKER, and it is the THIRD regression in four generations of the same two patterns. The reviewer built a 1,992-form corpus, pulled every generation's regexes straight from the git blobs, and computed the set "denied by a predecessor, allowed by this one". It was not empty: writing the run-END as a LIST of terminators (space/;/&/quote) is short by cmd.exe's own `>`, `)` and `,`, so `rd <dir> /s/q>nul` and `if exist <dir> (rd <dir> /s/q)` ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ about as idiomatic as batch gets ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ walked through and DELETED their targets on live-fire, along with the `,` form and the del equivalents. The sting: the PREVIOUS generation had caught them purely BY ACCIDENT, because its boundary class contained the path separator and so matched at a multi-segment run's inner separator without ever reaching the end. Removing that character on sound grammatical grounds silently removed the accident with it ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ which is exactly why a differential is not optional even when the reasoning has improved.
  FIXED by expressing the boundary as NOT A CONTINUATION rather than as a terminator list: `([^a-zA-Z/]|$)`. A one-letter switch segment must simply not be followed by another LETTER (that would be a multi-letter token cmd.exe refuses) or by the separator (already consumed by the segment run). There is no list left to be short by. Thirty-one destructive forms now deny on both twins; eight controls pass.
  THE REAL DELIVERABLE THIS ROUND IS ARM 4, THE DIFFERENTIAL ITSELF. The previous round's ratchet said to diff a loosening against its predecessor ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ and then that same round regressed, because the rule was a habit nobody performed. So the differential is now a committed, re-runnable ARM: it extracts all five generations' regexes from the git BLOBS (never transcribed ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a transcription slip would make the differential agree with itself), evaluates them over a generated 1,834-form corpus, and FAILS on any loss not on an adjudicated list that can only be extended by a live-fire verdict. Current run: 857 forms gained, 128 lost, all 128 adjudicated ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ two-letter tokens cmd.exe refuses (`/sq`, `/qs`, target survived both orders) and the deliberate false-positive removals. It carries a POSITIVE CONTROL on its own filter, because "no unadjudicated losses" reassures only if the filter can still report one: the six round-4 regressions are checked against the adjudication pattern every run and must survive it, so an over-broad pattern fails loudly instead of silently swallowing the next regression. Its extractor also refuses to run if it reads anything other than exactly two patterns per generation ├óÔé¼ÔÇØ a differential that silently reads zero proves nothing, and that guard fired for real on the first attempt.
